 the average person in the uk now  will work for 11 different employers in  their  lifetime so i'm going to  fire a question at you harriet we've  obviously been talking about  um  retirement planning for largely for  middle-class folk who are not too badly  off here but shmuel asked us what's your  solution to financial security for  retired people from low  socioeconomic groups so let you know  just for a moment let's address people  who haven't got the half million pot or  much much smaller they might own  their house  that may be their only substantial asset  where would where do you start  well i think again go back to the state  pension and the strengthening that we've  put in place with the state pension i  know that um over the three year time  horizon it will have been triple locked  and it will have protected against these  rises in inflation uh that we've seen  for people who are relying on that basic  state pension the other thing i would  really like to get out as a very strong  message is there are so many people in  this country i think it's 850 000 people  on low incomes who are entitled to  pension credit who do not actually  register for pension credit so there's  money just sitting there for them that  they're not asking for so i would  recommend uh people do a benefits check  to see if there are benefits they could  be accessing um obviously as you get  older you um  or get the the money in terms of your  your heating allowance that's very  important um but also in your local  authority they may have a discretionary  fund that can help you that is perhaps  administered in as in my area by the  citizens advice so there are a range of  different schemes that do help people on  low incomes but my central message would  be about making sure you're claiming  that pension credit you're entitled to  and  should you go to the citizens advice  bureau should should your constituent  come to your surgery to get the details  either would be fine and there are some  quite good uh benefit checkers on the on  the dwp website like turn to us and  things like that which will um allow you  to put in your details and we'll give  you some some signposting  yeah  i'm sure it's all there online somewhere  oh i know i know well then  go and see your friendly local mp yeah  yeah sitting in front of them yeah yeah  we've got we've got six or seven minutes  left we'll do one or two more quick  questions so charlotte any comment david  asks on splitting money with one spouse  assuming you're staying married yes yeah  um  so so actually that's a very good  question because one of the things we've  talked a little bit about today is that  there are  definite tax allowances that we should  all be taking advantage of so  pensions  isis  the two probably most important ones and  often we find that people haven't  thought that if their spouse hasn't  maxed out on those areas that they  should contribute into them as well so  assuming you're intending to to remain  married which is you know always always  positive um it is definitely worth  making sure that if you have a  non-working spouse or somebody who  stopped working you know for whatever  reason that that you are helping max out  on their pension  same to max out on their ices  um and then i would say the other thing  to think about is even if you want to  keep your finances separate which is  some what some couples choose to do and  is completely understandable it is worth  thinking about if you're going to have  financial advice or you're going to have  your money managed by somebody to think  about that  together even though you may remain two  independent clients because you will you  know certainly in our case um we have  something called the net wealth network  and you build economies of scale across  a family or across actually friends if  you want which allows you then to all  benefit from lower fees so i you know i  think um the sort of the spousal  awareness of how you can 